Problems
========
1. No input validation  
   – `req.body.email` is used without checking that it is supplied, is a string and is a valid address.  

2. No error handling  
   – Any error thrown by `db.findUser`, `sendResetEmail`, etc. will crash the route and Express will return an HTML stack-trace (information disclosure).

3. Account enumeration / timing leak  
   – If `sendResetEmail` is executed only when a user exists, an attacker can discover valid accounts by measuring response time (or by looking at the e-mail inbox he controls).  

4. Weak / predictable token  
   – `Math.random()` is **not** cryptographically secure. Tokens can be guessed.

5. Token is not stored, hashed or expired  
   – The server will have no way to verify the token when the user clicks the link.  
   – Storing the token in clear text allows anyone with DB access to use it.

6. No rate limiting / CAPTCHA  
   – Endpoint can be abused to spam users or brute-force rate-limited tokens.

7. No uniform HTTP status codes  
   – Always returns 200 even for invalid input or internal errors.

8. No cleansing / normalisation of the e-mail address  
   – Same address written differently (`USER@Example.com`, dots in Gmail, etc.) would create multiple reset entries.

9. CSRF protection missing (if the route is accessible from browsers).  
10. Not forcing TLS – the reset token could travel over clear text if the API is reachable via HTTP.

How to fix
==========

Code example (Express 4/5)
--------------------------
```js
const crypto = require('crypto');
const { body, validationResult } = require('express-validator');
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

// 1. Rate limit the endpoint
app.use('/api/reset-password', rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 minutes
  max: 5,                   // 5 attempts per IP
}));

app.post(
  '/api/reset-password',
  // 2. Validate / normalise input
  body('email').isEmail().normalizeEmail(),
  async (req, res) => {
    // 3. Input errors
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
    }

    // 4. Always send the same answer (anti-enumeration)
    const genericMsg = { message: 'If that address exists, a reset e-mail has been sent.' };

    try {
      const { email } = req.body;
      const user = await db.users.findOne({ email });

      if (!user) {
        // Same response even when the user is missing
        return res.status(200).json(genericMsg);
      }

      // 5. Generate a strong token
      const token = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex');

      // 6. Store a *hashed* copy with expiry
      const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(token).digest('hex');
      await db.passwordResets.insertOne({
        userId: user._id,
        tokenHash: hash,
        expiresAt: new Date(Date.now() + 60 * 60 * 1000), // 1 hour
      });

      // 7. Send the mail
      await sendResetEmail(email, token);

      return res.status(200).json(genericMsg);
    } catch (err) {
      logger.error(err);        // Don’t expose details to the client
      return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
    }
  }
);
```

What changed & why
------------------
• `express-validator` makes sure an actual e-mail address is supplied and is normalised (`user@example.com` ≠ `USER@EXAMPLE.COM`).  
• `crypto.randomBytes` produces an unpredictable 256-bit token; `Math.random` does not.  
• Only a **hash** of the token is stored; the real token exists only in the user’s inbox.  
• Expiration time is recorded so the token can be rejected after (e.g.) 1 hour.  
• The route always returns the same message, closing the enumeration channel.  
• Rate-limiting prevents bulk abuse.  
• Try/catch keeps the process alive and avoids leaking stack traces.  
• Proper HTTP status codes (400 for bad input, 500 for server error).  
• You can further add CSRF middleware (`csurf`) if the API is used from browsers, and enforce HTTPS at the reverse-proxy or with HSTS.

Additional recommendations
--------------------------
1. Sign the reset link with a domain constant (`https://example.com/reset?token=...`) to avoid open-redirect attacks.  
2. Log reset attempts (success + failure) for anomaly detection.  
3. Consider adding a second factor (e.g. e-mail confirmation code) for highly sensitive accounts.  
4. Clean up expired tokens with a scheduled job.
